  "diagnosis_text": "The current path is h1\u2013s4\u2013s2\u2013s1\u2013s3\u2013s6\u2013h5, and all its inter-switch links are active. I\u2019ll measure connectivity and TCP throughput, then check for congestion or loss along that path.\nThe slow transfer is reproduced: h1\u2192h5 achieved only 0.20 Mbit/s, with about 105 ms average ping RTT. So far, s2\u2019s port 1 toward s1 is the only sampled forward-path port showing a queue backlog and repeated queue overlimit events. I\u2019ll compare transfers that avoid this link and check its reverse direction.\n**The bottleneck is localised to s2 port 1, transmitting toward s1 port 1.** The evidence strongly suggests egress rate limiting/shaping with persistent queueing.\n\n- **Symptom reproduced:** h1\u2192h5 TCP throughput was **0.20 Mbit/s**. Ping averaged **105 ms**, with no loss in five probes.\n- **Bottleneck evidence:** Two separate samples of s2\u2192s1 showed approximately **1 Mbit/s** transmitted, **10\u201311 packets queued**, and **364\u2013369 queue overlimit events per two seconds**. Overlimit events indicate queue rate enforcement, not necessarily packet drops.\n- **Other forward-path ports sampled:** No backlog, overlimit events, drops, or errors.\n- **Control tests avoiding s2\u2013s1:** h1\u2192h3 and h7\u2192h5 achieved approximately **33 Gbit/s** in this virtual network, arguing against a general h1 or h5 performance problem.\n- **Directionality:** h5\u2192h1 delivered **12.66 Mbit/s**, substantially faster than the forward transfer, but not comparable to the bypass tests. Its acknowledgements still traverse the constrained s2\u2192s1 direction.\n\n**Next step:** Inspect the Linux traffic-control/qdisc configuration on **`s2-eth1`**, especially shaping rates and queue limits, and identify traffic sharing that egress. The repeated ~1 Mbit/s measurements suggest a cap around that rate, but the configured limit and traffic sources are not directly verified by these tools. I have made no changes.",
